name: network-interception
description: The Spectra.intercept() and Spectra.spy() APIs in TestSpectra — InterceptRule/MockResponse/MockHandle shapes, respondOnce vs respondWith sequential polling, aborting requests, waiting for a real API with spy().waitForResponse(), and waitForCall/shouldHaveCallCount/shouldHaveStatus/shouldHaveRequestBody assertions. Use whenever a test needs to wait for, mock, stub, or assert on an API call.
---

# Skill: Network Interception (`Spectra.intercept`)

Use this skill whenever a test needs deterministic control over a network response — mocking an
API, simulating an error, or asserting a call happened — rather than depending on a real backend.

---

## 1. Rule & Handle Shapes

```typescript
interface InterceptRule {
  url: string | RegExp; // a glob matches the whole URL (a query may follow), e.g. '**/api/v1/payments/charge'
  method?: 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE' | 'PATCH' | 'ALL';
  response?: MockResponse | ((request: InterceptedRequest) => MockResponse | Promise<MockResponse>);
}

interface MockResponse {
  status?: number; // default: 200
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  body?: any;
  delayMs?: number;
}

interface MockHandle {
  respondWith(response: MockResponse): this; // default response for all subsequent matches
  respondOnce(response: MockResponse): this; // one-time FIFO-queued response
  abort(errorCode?: 'Failed' | 'Aborted' | 'TimedOut' | 'ConnectionReset'): this;
  waitForCall(options?: { timeout?: number; count?: number }): Promise<InterceptedRequest>;
  waitForResponse(options?: { timeout?: number; count?: number }): Promise<CDPNetworkEntry>; // the answer delivered, after delayMs
  shouldHaveCallCount(count: number, options?: { timeout?: number }): Promise<void>; // polls; 0 proves no request went out
  shouldHaveStatus(status: number, options?: { count?: number; timeout?: number }): Promise<void>; // default: the last answer
  shouldHaveRequestBody(expected: string | object, options?: { timeout?: number }): Promise<void>; // object: JSON subset; string: substring
  shouldHaveResponseBody(expected: string | object, options?: { count?: number; timeout?: number }): Promise<void>; // the answer's body; default: the last
  readonly callCount: number;
  readonly calls: InterceptedRequest[];
}

interface InterceptedRequest {
  url: string;
  method: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  postData?: string; // the request body
}
```

A function `response` runs for each matching request and may forward it, so the real server
answers late (the page waits `delayMs` for the reply):

```typescript
await Spectra.intercept({
  url: '**/api/v1/brands/*',
  method: 'PATCH',
  response: async (request) => {
    const reply = await fetch(request.url, {
      method: 'PATCH',
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: request.postData,
    });
    return { status: reply.status, body: await reply.text(), delayMs: 3000 };
  },
});
```

`Spectra.intercept(rule)` is `async` and returns a `MockHandle` — always `await` it before
interacting with the UI that triggers the call.

---

## 2. Pattern: Single-Shot Static Mock

```typescript
it('should mock payment failure response gracefully', async () => {
  const paymentMock = await Spectra.intercept({
    url: '**/api/v1/payments/charge',
    method: 'POST',
    response: {
      status: 402,
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: { error: 'insufficient_funds', message: 'Your card was declined due to insufficient funds.' },
    },
  });

  await CheckoutPage.submitPaymentButton.click();

  await paymentMock.waitForCall({ timeout: 5000 });
  await paymentMock.shouldHaveCallCount(1);

  await CheckoutPage.errorBanner.shouldBeVisible();
  await CheckoutPage.errorBanner.shouldContainText('insufficient funds');
});
```

---

## 3. Pattern: Multi-Response Sequential Polling

Use `respondOnce` (FIFO queue, consumed one per matching request) plus a trailing `respondWith`
default for status-polling / retry flows:

```typescript
it('should poll export job status until completed', async () => {
  const jobMock = await Spectra.intercept({ url: '**/api/v1/export/job-99', method: 'GET' });

  jobMock.respondOnce({ status: 200, body: { status: 'pending' } });
  jobMock.respondOnce({ status: 200, body: { status: 'processing' } });
  jobMock.respondWith({ status: 200, body: { status: 'completed', downloadUrl: '/files/report.pdf' } });

  await ExportPage.startExportButton.click();
  await ExportPage.downloadLink.shouldBeVisible();
});
```

---

## 4. Pattern: Simulating a Network Failure

```typescript
it('should handle offline mode gracefully when sync fails', async () => {
  const syncMock = await Spectra.intercept({ url: '**/api/v1/sync' });
  syncMock.abort('ConnectionReset');

  await DashboardPage.syncButton.click();
  await DashboardPage.offlineBanner.shouldBeVisible();
});
```

---

## 5. Pattern: Waiting for an API (`Spectra.spy`)

Wait for an API with a spy, never with a guessed duration (`Spectra.sleep`). The spy leaves the
request alone: the real server answers. Make it before the step that sends the request.

```typescript
const verify = await Spectra.spy('**/v2/iam/auth/otp/verify', 'POST'); // method default: 'ALL'
await OtpPage.boxes.setValue('000000');
await verify.waitForResponse(); // default timeout: the implicit wait
await verify.shouldHaveStatus(422);
await verify.shouldHaveRequestBody({ code: '000000' });
await verify.shouldHaveResponseBody({ reason: 'invalid_code' }); // the response body: JSON subset or substring
await OtpPage.error.shouldHaveText('Invalid code');
```

The spy handle has the same `waitForResponse` / `shouldHaveCallCount` / `shouldHaveStatus` /
`shouldHaveRequestBody` / `shouldHaveResponseBody` / `callCount` / `calls` as above. Only requests
answered after the spy is made count. `requestBody` and `responseBody` are cut to 500 characters:
expect a part of a long body as a string. `responseBody` is recorded for text Fetch/XHR responses
only; any other fails the assertion saying why.

A live update over SSE (`new EventSource(url)`, web e2e): spy on the stream's URL before the page
opens it, then assert the event and what the UI made of it. `data` matches as a substring (string)
or a JSON subset (object); `event` is the event's name (`message` when the server names none):

```typescript
const stream = await Spectra.spy('**/api/live-updates**');
await Spectra.browser.url('/settings');
await stream.shouldHaveReceivedEvent({ event: 'settings-updated', data: { key: 'timezone' } });
await SettingsPage.timezone.shouldHaveText('Asia/Jakarta');
stream.serverEvents(); // [{ event, id, data, receivedAt }]
```

Events count from the moment the spy is made, on a stream already open too. To prove the page got
no event (another tenant's change), watch a window: it fails at the first matching event.

```typescript
const live = await Spectra.spy('**/api/live-updates**'); // the stream the page already opened
await Step.changeTimezoneAsOtherTenant('Asia/Tokyo');
await live.shouldNotHaveReceivedEvent({ event: 'settings-updated' }, { during: 3000 });
```

A spy watches every tab. When two tabs open the same URL (tenant A in tab 0, tenant B in an
isolated tab 1), watch one with `{ tab }`, the index `switchToTab` takes, fixed when the spy is made:

```typescript
const tenantB = await Spectra.spy('**/api/live-updates**', { tab: 1 }); // or (pattern, 'GET', { tab: 1 })
```

Intercept with `delayMs` only when the step reads the page while the request is in flight; then
`waitForResponse()` before checking the result:

```typescript
const save = await Spectra.intercept({
  url: '**/api/v1/brands',
  method: 'POST',
  response: { status: 201, body: {}, delayMs: 3000 },
});
await BrandPage.saveButton.click();
await BrandPage.saveButton.shouldBeDisabled(); // read mid-request
await save.waitForResponse();
await BrandPage.toast.shouldHaveText('Brand created');
```

---

## 6. Conventions

- Prefer glob URL patterns (`'**/api/v1/...'`) over exact origin strings so mocks survive
  environment/base-URL changes between local, staging, and CI.
- A glob matches the whole URL: `'**/api/v1/brands'` does not also catch `/api/v1/brands/check-availability`; end it with `*` to cover every path under it.
- Only mock what the test is actually about — don't blanket-intercept every domain; the linter and
  reviewers expect `monitoredDomains` in `spectra.config.ts` (see the `workspace-structure` skill)
  to reflect real traffic, and over-mocking hides real integration regressions.
- Wait for an API with `Spectra.spy(...).waitForResponse()`, not a guessed duration. Intercept +
  `delayMs` only when the step reads the page mid-request, then `waitForResponse()` before checking the result.
- Always assert on the mock (`waitForCall` / `shouldHaveCallCount(n)`) when the test's purpose is to
  verify the request was made — don't just assert on UI state and silently hope the mock fired. A
  blocked submit: `shouldHaveCallCount(0)` after the `should*` on the state the app settles in.
- Every real (non-mocked) network call made during a run is separately captured and written to
  `.testspectra/reports/network-resources.json` for post-run diagnostics — you don't need to record
  this yourself.

